\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{budget_curve}
\caption{Accuracy as a function of token budget and truncation direction (Qwen-2.5-7B, $N{=}1{,}190$). Tail-truncation (keeping last $k$ tokens) monotonically improves beyond 256 tokens and consistently outperforms head-truncation by 13--19pp at budgets ${\geq}$512. The flat head-truncation curve (77--82\%) contrasts with the steep tail-truncation rise, confirming that diagnostic signal is localized near trace end. Dashed lines show \texttt{exception\_only} (99.83\%) and \texttt{no\_trace} (75.13\%) as upper and lower bounds. Tail-256 (${\approx}$ \texttt{no\_trace}) indicates the signal window begins approximately 256 tokens from the trace terminus.}
\label{fig:trace_length}
\end{figure}

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:trace_length} and Figure~\ref{fig:trace_length}, all four trace length conditions fall within a 5pp range (77.2\%--81.8\%).

\paragraph{Truncation direction and budget curve.}
Tail-truncation (retaining the last $k$ tokens) dramatically outperforms head-truncation: among the three models with complete data, tail-2048 exceeds head-2048 by 15.5--22.6pp, with the gap widening at smaller scale (Qwen-1.5B: +22.6pp vs.\ Qwen-7B: +15.5pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:trace_length_full}, Table~\ref{tab:head_vs_tail}).
All four models' head-2048 accuracies converge into a narrow 75.3--81.8\% band (6.5pp), comparable to the tail-256 band (72.6--78.9\%, 6.3pp), indicating that signal-poor conditions produce a shared performance floor regardless of scale or architecture.

A cross-model budget sweep across four models and three architecture families reveals a sharp phase transition at 256$\to$512 tail tokens: all four models jump +14.7--23.0pp in a single budget doubling, crossing from near-baseline (${\sim}$73--79\%) to near-ceiling (${\sim}$94--98\%).
The threshold aligns with the typical exception traceback length (${\sim}$500 tokens): below it, the diagnostic event is truncated; above it, the event is captured regardless of architecture or scale.
Beyond 512 tokens, accuracy converges further; where tail-1024 data is available, three models reach a 96.5--96.6\% band, eliminating the 6.3pp cross-model spread observed at tail-256 (Table~\ref{tab:tail_cross_model}).
\texttt{tail\_2048} captures 95--98\% of \texttt{exception\_only}'s accuracy across all completed configurations.
The residual 2--5pp gap confirms that semantic extraction (identifying exception type and message) still outperforms positional extraction (retaining a fixed window containing non-diagnostic lines).

